{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1082", "sample_idx": 24, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "The morning sun, a blinding white circle, assaulted Arthur’s eyes. He blinked, disoriented, and sat up in bed. Except, the bed wasn’t his bed. It was…different. The floral pattern was too vibrant, the sheets too smooth, and a faint, almost imperceptible hum vibrated through the mattress. He glanced around the room, a small, pastel-colored space with oversized furniture and a strangely perfect arrangement of knick-knacks. His reflection in the mirrored closet door showed a man he vaguely recognized – but the features were…sharper, more defined. He wasn't wearing his usual worn flannel pajamas, but a crisp, white button-down shirt. A creeping unease began to coil in his stomach. He reached for his glasses, only to find them inexplicably on his face. This wasn't his apartment. This wasn’t even his world.\n\n***\n\nPanic clawed at Arthur’s throat. He stumbled out of bed, the floor feeling strangely springy beneath his feet. The hallway was equally bizarre, all pristine white and impossibly clean. A digital chime echoed from a nearby door, and he saw, through the peephole, a perfectly rendered scene of a cheerful, pixelated family waving. A notification popped up in his vision – a translucent overlay that read: \"Welcome to Aethelgard! Level 1 Initiated.\" Level 1? Aethelgard? He touched the overlay, and the world shimmered, the colors intensifying, the textures becoming hyper-realistic. He realized, with a chilling certainty, that he wasn't dreaming. He wasn't hallucinating. He was inside a video game. And he had no idea how to get out.\n\n\n\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "The", "logprob": -0.6729406118392944}, {"token": "▁morning", "logprob": -1.5677294731140137}, {"token": "▁sun", "logprob": -0.07833115756511688}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.9950055480003357}, {"token": "▁a", "logprob": -0.1478339284658432}, {"token": "▁blinding", "logprob": -0.47908914089202